{"id": "clm:beb8779430a5ed17cf5649486d03124619926963d7e2840a7ea1b257b29497a9", "type": "statement", "text": "Shadow traffic is not automatically side-effect-free. A state-changing request may execute on both versions, and shared state such as a cache can alter or contaminate performance measurements. Isolate candidate data and downstream effects, or make replayed operations safe to repeat. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "definition", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:087fbbe22fcde1fec76331597c12050385fa159ed14794e06f85d14d7d3b8539", "ev:12b0b69cc8dedf1c3c890f49ae4e41e205c6c2ec61d32618f8df5bac48ec175b"]}
{"id": "clm:e61d8e11b246f2900c762ed7a98a895b3e9d8b7ad57f1b975f829b58e0cb8f58", "type": "statement", "text": "A canary routes a controlled share of production traffic to a changed version while the control serves the rest. Users in the canary slice receive candidate responses, so defects can affect that slice; teams compare candidate and control signals and can stop or roll back when the candidate regresses. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/automated-canary-analysis-at-netflix-with-kayenta-3260bc7acc69", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/", "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:820cc0058b47b46f94f99ee181d41bfe978fc24143d806ab890f5f2b69f20538", "ev:fdcdbfe48547564555f300ae143f3f8e1edf03ef09bb0e45d89d6c559b9275ca"]}
{"id": "clm:5f9201af41b3fc14de4008ab253429af9590444f18d965085addbc2b7920d0a7", "type": "statement", "text": "AWS VPC Traffic Mirroring is not universally zero-impact: AWS documentation says mirrored packets count toward source-instance bandwidth and may be dropped when bandwidth or packet-rate limits are exceeded. The AWS blog's no-performance-impact statement is specific to its out-of-band architecture and should not be generalized to every mirroring setup. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-10-23)", "source_url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/mirroring/traffic-mirroring-network-limitations.html", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:84c125c6f76e918c945b7afbf42e722e7104bc8ab74b48d7588885c2a117aa11", "ev:85b39ae41c6b0be0e3bc4cf5ea676622f080723ac5eb6d1a101deb2fa42e96d3"]}
{"id": "clm:19bcee24259a07d705262a6fdcb5a43365af300ef3eb265f544023bb475cde35", "type": "statement", "text": "Shadow runs exercise the candidate on copied production inputs but cannot show how users respond to candidate answers because only the incumbent response reaches them. Replayed state-changing requests can duplicate effects; AWS calls out idempotency, and InfoQ recommends read-only replicas or staging data for downstream state. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.infoq.com/articles/microservices-traffic-mirroring-istio-vpc/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:26092bc7d9a8103b9a0b1943d169d8d10f6c099afaec62a989d9ecc2b61986f3", "ev:8658e7e41985ad6a9bf633830bfd47f2397f4ff56284127b7423c4a6258af216", "ev:f2fd2d717b2ed00fa2acfd8bcb6b54f39df85264ed057e9349e50e6fb91baa90"]}
{"id": "clm:7085fa7483c1235e8603e3e0669f7e87ba213e457f2ad58f9554441dbd677e52", "type": "statement", "text": "In Envoy v1.36.11, RequestMirrorPolicy mirrors all requests when runtime_fraction is unset and can restrict mirroring to a configured fraction when it is set. Sampling can limit candidate load, with the trade-off that fewer requests are measured. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-10-01)", "source_url": "https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/v1.36.11/api-v3/config/route/v3/route_components.proto", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "comparisons", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:303739af61db2d86296daea63d101ba8cc161774a3b21766fcceb4c535b278a2"]}
{"id": "clm:86a920c781a082d3e14831675ea854471863a4e10053b934e847d7b640360853", "type": "statement", "text": "A canary routes a portion of live requests to a candidate, so some users receive candidate responses; shadow traffic copies production requests to the candidate while the incumbent serves the client and the shadow response is discarded. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/automated-canary-analysis-at-netflix-with-kayenta-3260bc7acc69", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:319e8c8a9f0d28341814aeb6c94c36585a773ec148390150058c693683916770"]}
{"id": "clm:274c4502d817efff2a252e0afac82f7d56f5289105fd61ed7770c2f73b41b40d", "type": "statement", "text": "A useful canary comparison uses a baseline running the production version and configuration, created alongside the candidate; match deployment time, instance count, traffic type, and traffic amount. Comparing against long-running production can confound results through cache warmup, heap, or load-balancing differences. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/canary-analysis-lessons-learned-and-best-practices-from-google-and-waze", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/automated-canary-analysis-at-netflix-with-kayenta-3260bc7acc69"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:d3b96c791dee7c823cc2f2a932a26de20c397986fa3a750624aa168fcbb8a2f6"]}
{"id": "clm:996b44e8f55d1b142b9c48a2887d565f77996f193ff94cf3422f9f73879c5a40", "type": "statement", "text": "Canary analysis should compare configured baseline and candidate time-series metrics that represent service health, including latency, errors, and saturation. Netflix's Kayenta description validates and cleans metric data, classifies individual metrics, and aggregates results; it describes Mann–Whitney U confidence intervals as its primary comparison algorithm. (high confidence) (verified as of 2018-04-10)", "source_url": "https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/automated-canary-analysis-at-netflix-with-kayenta-3260bc7acc69", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/introducing-kayenta-an-open-automated-canary-analysis-tool-from-google-and-netflix", "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/canary-analysis-lessons-learned-and-best-practices-from-google-and-waze"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:6ad5ee2927ec87fd44775e05e66cc985007c5347ee9a0a6ac90ebc0635b199a7", "ev:e23d9faeb03696c0945d9794c8b87bffd24aaf3a47a45f463f8cc9aea7e8ccbd"]}
{"id": "clm:7b0b7218474c1a25bd74ae33dd866582732eae7977d3c1b2ff5e9239333fd954", "type": "statement", "text": "Google and Waze recommend validating a canary analysis configuration with known-good and known-bad app versions, and running an A/A check with the same version on both sides that should pass. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/canary-analysis-lessons-learned-and-best-practices-from-google-and-waze", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:d1eab21a258876f0b2441e1785e630208cee2b107dcf392671df214c87dc60a7"]}
{"id": "clm:52228740b56c530f3aecd155b3c04596260c99387dd198fbf835145b032829f7", "type": "statement", "text": "Google and Waze's Spinnaker guidance recommends at least 50 time-series data points per metric for statistically relevant canary analysis; depending on monitoring granularity, this can require several hours of analysis. (low confidence) (verified as of 2019-01-14)", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/canary-analysis-lessons-learned-and-best-practices-from-google-and-waze", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:61efef5619169eaa8eb00971d9ee54df87b396193c6bef188113e814c6f5b44c"]}
{"id": "clm:61bf77d09c35867e08844351ade220d91daa8ae1bab4a6778ecc3ed186f8875f", "type": "statement", "text": "Shadowing leaves the stable version on the client response path and sends a copy of each selected request to the candidate; the candidate response is discarded and does not delay the primary response. This exercises the candidate on real request inputs without exposing its output to users. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/mirror-production-traffic-to-test-environment-with-vpc-traffic-mirroring/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/v1.36.11/api-v3/config/route/v3/route_components.proto", "https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:07e5c4f6f6459c634aca6b64b2ae593c94cfd750cdc4bccafdac1979e5d5b9ec", "ev:12b0b69cc8dedf1c3c890f49ae4e41e205c6c2ec61d32618f8df5bac48ec175b", "ev:380f4c6b348263ed3dde6dd1e963873f3ca2bb8374f6f7284d0eb8223fef49a6"]}
{"id": "clm:5efe7eeed0db781066ad989d8593cb42e251cecd94b5779d3de051d7da1e5ed5", "type": "statement", "text": "Canary metrics should be broken down by candidate and control population, and their aggregation intervals should fit within the canary duration. Service-wide metrics can dilute a regression affecting a small traffic slice, while a time window longer than the canary can mix unrelated events into the comparison. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Canary and Shadow-Traffic Comparison"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/automated-canary-analysis-at-netflix-with-kayenta-3260bc7acc69"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:b3949bcc14285178d1772f8346c4b006bfb645976367e5086bba6e80f766f2e4", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:cb45864051d5bedde2d416e2f9192de48835c268253703777f5e4954b137159c"]}
